## Testing tools and processes

General guidance: [Testing for accessibility (Service Manual)](https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/helping-people-to-use-your-service/testing-for-accessibility).

The Service Manual talks about four types of testing: automated testing, manual testing, testing with assistive technologies and including people with access needs in user research.


### Automated testing

Automated testing tools (choose one):

* [Axe](https://www.deque.com/axe/) (the team uses jest-axe for continuous integration as well as html-validate)
* [WAVE](http://wave.webaim.org/extension/)
* [ARC Toolkit](https://www.tpgi.com/arc-platform/arc-toolkit/)
* [SiteImprove](https://www.siteimprove.com/integrations/browser-extensions/)
* browser accessibility reports (for example, Lighthouse in Chrome's devtools)


### Manual testing

How to use [accessibility acceptance criteria](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-frontend/blob/main/docs/contributing/test-components-using-accessibility-acceptance-criteria.md) for the GOV.UK Design System.

Checklists for basic and easier checks (choose one):

* [Doing a basic accessibility check if you cannot do a detailed one (GOV.UK guidance)](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/doing-a-basic-accessibility-check-if-you-cant-do-a-detailed-one/doing-a-basic-accessibility-check-if-you-cant-do-a-detailed-one)
* [Easy Checks (W3C)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/preliminary/)
* [How to do a quick accessibility review (internal Word doc)](https://beisgov.sharepoint.com/:w:/s/ServiceTransformation/IQCTdKO0-Mk-Rrcmh1M0rvHJAfc4eyxGDAnvcnjEzhCD7UY?e=YjmlXo)

Checklists for more involved checks (choose one):

* [Accessibility testing guide (GDS)](https://github.com/alphagov/wcag-primer/wiki)
* [Accessibility Insights](https://accessibilityinsights.io/)


### Testing with assistive technologies (AT)

General guidance: [Testing with assistive technologies (Service Manual)](https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/technology/testing-with-assistive-technologies).

If you don't have access to the AT listed in the Service Manual, test with these at a minimum:

* any desktop screen reader
* any mobile screen reader
* any screen magnifier
* any speech recognition software / voice control tool

You can get access to most AT via [Assistiv Labs](https://gds-way.digital.cabinet-office.gov.uk/manuals/accessibility.html#using-assistiv-labs-to-test-with-assistive-technologies) and [BrowserStack](https://gds-way.digital.cabinet-office.gov.uk/manuals/browser-support.html#testing).

What neither [BrowserStack supports](https://www.browserstack.com/docs/live/accessibility-testing) nor [Assistiv Labs support](https://assistivlabs.com/settings/pricing) are:

* VoiceOver on iOS - You have to use your work phone for testing
* Dragon (or any other voice control tool) - You have to ask Anika to test (or hope for the Accessibility Lab to re-open)

Record results on a copy of [our accessibility testing spreadsheet](https://beisgov.sharepoint.com/:x:/s/GOVUKDS/IQAMnTltG8V8RZxiCrhYbooxAa7fU9J3jgYuB0PHw_c87MI?e=lUUUMK).


### User Research

General guidance: [Running research sessions with disabled people (Service Manual)](https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/running-research-sessions-with-people-with-disabilities).

Not the same as user research but very useful is a cognitive walkthrough with the help of [our accessibility personas](https://alphagov.github.io/accessibility-personas/).


